t \"Unauthorized\". The only differences I can spot are the JSON payload size and content complexity, so maybe the server is rejecting oversized requests or there's something specific about how the complex JSON is being parsed. I see the issue now—I was passing the JSON object directly as the `-d` parameter with single quotes, but the shell might have interpreted the newlines or braces differently than expected. The POST with the simple test message worked fine with the same header format, so the API key is being recognized. I should try sending the actual content message again, perhaps encoding it differently to ensure the JSON parses correctly on the server side. I'm wondering if there's a size constraint on the request body that's causing the failure, or perhaps the server has some inconsistency in how it validates the API key depending on the content. Let me try a smaller message first to isolate whether this is a payload size issue. I need to verify whether the first POST attempt actually went through—the one with the complex JSON structure. If it did succeed, I might have duplicated messages. Let me check the recent message history to see what actually reached the server, then decide whether to retry or just move forward with sending smaller messages.\n\nGiven the time constraints, I'll attempt one more POST with a more compact payload to see if that resolves the issue.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nPOST 用 X-API-Key 成功了（simple test 发了出去）。让我重试带 X-API-Key 的 GET 并重新发送正式回复：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-1159876f-4d74-4b5f-ba91-59634cfbf14a","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"# 验证当前最新消息状态\ncurl -s -H \"X-API-Key: echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/history?threadId=arbitration&limit=2\" | \\\n  python3 -c \"import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); [print(m['id'], m['timestamp'], m['content'][:80]) for m in d['messages']]\""}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-bdb3b231-3b